const PART: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "np:0": [
    "The highest peak in the world has several names, and none of them means trophy. In Nepali it is called Sagarmatha. In Tibetan it is Chomolungma, goddess of the sky and mother of the world. Sherpas and Tibetans treat the mountain as a home, not a prize for planting a flag. Nepal, full of other enormous summits, teaches this with names spoken slowly. A child looking at the horizon still hears the word mother. Without that name, the stone is only height. With it, height becomes an altar, and an altar asks permission.",
    "The legend is simple and serious. If someone climbs, they climb with permission. If the goddess feels offended, she takes away the weather. At eight thousand metres, weather is not a game on a timer. It is the life of people breathing badly, who can still turn back down. The mother of the world does not want people trapped on the summit because of a record. She wants the neck to bend sometimes, and the climber to come down alive. It is the old way of saying that pride, at that height, costs far too much.",
    "In the Khumbu, a young guide watched the bad sky and heard the clients still asking for the summit. They had paid, they had waited, and the photographs were still to be taken. He remembered the name his grandmother used, mother, and he closed the day. There was no long speech. There was one sentence: ‘Today the mountain is not calling us.’ Some people were angry. Others thanked him later, with their tea in their hands. He had listened to the legend where it is useful: before the wrong step, not after.",
    "They went down. The village welcomed them with no easy laughter and no shame. The elders know that turning back, on a bad day, is a form of respect, not a defeat. The child who saw the group come in understood that the little flag is not the summit's only law. She asked whether the goddess had spoken. The guide said: ‘She spoke in the wind and in the cloud. I only translated.’ That night nobody invented a danger bigger than the one that was there. Sagarmatha was still up there, whole.",
    "Without the name of mother, Everest becomes a trophy and people become numbers. With it, the peak stays an altar, and an altar lets live those who treat it well. Nepal does not fit into one planted flag. It fits into a licence that was asked for, and a return in good time. The guide who refuses the summit is listening to the legend, even without old words. If you ever look at Sagarmatha, do not only ask how to get to the top. Ask also how to get back. The goddess measures the journey by the return, not the record.",
  ],
  "np:1": [
    "In the high mountains of Nepal, Sherpa and Tibetan folklore speaks of the yeti, the snowman. Some say it is a spirit. Others say it is a large mountain primate, a distant cousin of the forest animals. It leaves wide footprints, gives a whistle, and frightens the yaks, which move away with nobody pushing them. The gentle version, told to children, does not make it a monster on the hunt. It makes it a neighbour of the mountain who would rather not be seen. If the noise comes too close, it gets cross, like anyone whose home has lost its silence.",
    "Many Sherpas speak of the yeti with half a smile and half respect. They do not swear they saw it yesterday, and they do not laugh as if the mountains were a playground. Science looks for a bear, measures footprints and compares hairs. The myth looks for a neighbour. The two searches can exist without insulting each other. A child of the Khumbu learns early what their grandmother says. Maybe it exists, the way other lands speak of the people of the sea. Even so, nobody goes looking at night. Curiosity stays. The pretend expedition does not.",
    "One afternoon, the boy Tashi heard a short whistle and wanted to go and see. His grandfather held his sleeve calmly. ‘If it is the yeti, it has already said what it had to say. If it is the wind, the wind does not need you in the dark.’ They stayed at the door. The whistle did not return. In the morning, on the thin snow, there was a line of large marks, already melting in the sun. Nobody rushed to organise a hunt. Tashi put his hand beside a mark, without spoiling it, and his own fitted several times. Then they went back to the tea.",
    "The grandfather said the yeti gets cross with noisy people. It has no time for anyone who shouts on the trail or leaves rubbish. It also dislikes whoever treats the yak like a thing. It does not come down to the village to harm children who speak quietly. It breaks the calm, not the house. That is why the legend asks for a silent step, a short voice and an early return. Tashi understood that the grown-ups' half smile was not mockery. It was the way of living beside a mystery without turning it into a war. The ice has humour and a limit.",
    "Without the yeti, the high mountains would be a white desert. With it, every whistle reminds you that the mountain is not an empty stage. The Nepali child of the Khumbu keeps the rule like a lantern. Maybe, and even so you do not go out at night to look. Science may keep looking for the bear. The village keeps looking for the silence. Both things fit. If you ever go to those mountains, speak quietly. The neighbour, if it is there, would rather not be seen. The summit is not measured only by those who get the highest.",
  ],
  "np:2": [
    "In Kathmandu, in Durbar Square, there is a house of carved wood and a window the whole country knows. The Kumari lives there, a chosen girl in whom the goddess Taleju dwells for some years. This is not a made-up tale to send children to sleep. It is the religion of the Newar people, told with respect. The girl appears at the window, still, dressed in red. People look up and receive the blessing. They do not ask her to come down into the street like a neighbour. During that time, she carries a sky while still being small.",
    "The rite asks that her foot does not touch the ground outside. When she goes out, she is carried carefully, because the presence of the goddess has old rules. Long ago she blessed the king. Today she may bless whoever represents the country. A child in the square sees another child, almost their own age. They notice something difficult. The divine, in this city, can be small and mortal and still command respect. She is not a statue. She breathes. She gets tired. She looks. Catmandu puts its heart there, at the height of your eyes, where Taleju chose silence.",
    "The choice of the Kumari follows signs that the priests know. The family knows that the goddess's years do not last for ever. It does not let the girl become an object. She has a room, a name and a tiredness. The sky she carries is real for those who believe. It is heavy for those who look at her tenderly. In the square, a boy learns not to shout or point as if she were a show. His mother says: ‘Treat her as you treat a flame. Stay close, but do not grab it.’ The window opens for the right time, and then closes.",
    "When the girl grows up, the goddess withdraws. There is no punishment in that leaving. There is a return. The Kumari stops being one, and lives again as a girl, and another child is chosen for the window. The gods in this land know how to withdraw, and that is a big part of the story. The boy from the square may come back years later. He may meet the former Kumari walking on the ground, with her feet in ordinary dust. Nobody owes her a prison of eternal respect. She is owed the memory of a time when the sky fitted into a house of wood.",
    "Without the girl, Taleju would fall silent in that rite. Durbar Square would lose its heart at the window. With her, Nepal says a rare sentence. The sacred does not need to be enormous or distant to guide a people. It needs care. The child looking up does not see a cold idol. They see a girl their own age, dressed in red, completing a time that will end. If you go to Kathmandu, stay silent before that window. The blessing, if it comes, is not bought. When the goddess withdraws, the girl is a girl again.",
  ],
  "np:3": [
    "Swayambhunath stands high above Kathmandu, and everybody calls it the monkey temple. The rhesus monkeys are thought to be sacred. They sit on the steps, steal fruit, and know the easy fear of anyone carrying an open biscuit. They are not decoration. They are residents. The legend says they are not there by chance. They are the troops of Manjushri, the sage who changed the valley. Before the city, the elders say, Kathmandu was an enormous lake. In the water floated a lotus flower. From it the shrine was born by itself, with no hand of a mason.",
    "Manjushri looked at the lake and saw that people needed ground. With a sword that the story calls wisdom, he cut a gorge at the edge of the valley. The water went down, the lake emptied, and the land appeared, damp and new. It was not a blow against anyone. It was a cut in the rock so that life could sit down. The monkeys stayed on as helpers, guarding the memory of what had been water. Geology agrees with the myth on one big point. The valley really was a lake, so legend and science point to the same dry ground.",
    "A child climbs the steps with a biscuit in their pocket and loses it in two seconds. The monkey sits further up, very serious, eating as if it were collecting a tax. The grandmother does not run after the animal. She laughs and says: ‘That is the temple tax. They charge for the memory of the lake.’ The child is left without a snack and with a better story. At the top, the great white shrine seems still to float, like the ancient lotus. The painted eyes look over the valley. Kathmandu, below, is the dry miracle.",
    "If Manjushri had not opened the valley, there would be no square for the Kumari. There would be no market, and none of these worn steps. The sword is not for a war. It is for turning water into a city. The city must remember the price: the lake did not come back. That is why the monkeys charge in fruit and in patience. Whoever climbs up angry comes down angrier. Whoever climbs up with a respectful laugh comes down telling the tax. The lotus at the top still teaches that sacred things sometimes are born alone.",
    "The child of Kathmandu keeps the next biscuit and offers a piece on purpose. It is better than losing it in a fright. It is a small peace with the troops of Manjushri. Without the sage, the valley would be water. With the cut in the mountain, it is a city. Without the monkeys, the temple would be only a view. With them, the view has inhabitants and a sense of humour. Swayambhu, up high, still looks like a flower resting on the old lake. If you go there, climb slowly and keep your snack closed.",
  ],
  "np:4": [
    "In Pokhara, Lake Fewa lies still in the morning, and the Annapurna comes down to the water as a reflection. There is a small island with a small temple. Anyone who rows there may make a wish. The legend adds an ancient snake, guardian of the bottom. It does not come up to frighten anyone who speaks quietly. In the story it rises only when someone wants to tear the lake open out of greed. Pokhara lives on lakes and on ledges where people stop without saying anything. The miracle is the peak appearing twice.",
    "It is said that a king had a chest of gold. He had the chest carried to the middle of Fewa. When the sun rose, he saw the Annapurna lying on the water. It was so clear that the gold seemed heavy and small. He said to the boatmen: ‘Some treasures weigh less than a reflection. I choose the view.’ He left the chest at the bottom. He did not want it back richer. He did not want anyone to drain the lake looking for it. He asked the snake to guard the gold like a secret that no longer matters.",
    "The snake agreed, on one condition. If anyone came with hooks and nets to drag out the chest, the water would turn cloudy. Then the reflection would die. If anyone came by boat, with empty hands, asking for a fair wish, the temple would listen. The lake would stay transparent. The king went back to the shore lighter. He was not really poor. He was poor in hidden gold. The boatmen told of the choice in the village. Some laughed. The elders did not. They knew mornings when the mountain seems within reach.",
    "A child rows with their father to the island, slowly, so as not to break the drawing of the Annapurna. They ask for a small wish: a safe way home, and not a chest. The snake does not show itself. The temple is enough. On the way back, the child sees the mountain twice and understands the ancient king without needing the gold. If the lake dried up because of a box, Pokhara would lose the mirror that makes it itself. The father says the bottom may keep whatever it likes. The surface is enough for us when it tells the truth.",
    "This is one of the clearest Nepali morals. Some treasures weigh less than a reflection. Some victories spoil the very thing they wanted to keep. Without the lake, the Annapurna would be only height. With the lake, the height learns to lie down and let itself be looked at. Fewa is a mirror that chose to be deep. The temple is the dot in the middle of the wish. If you go to Pokhara, row without greed. Ask for little. Leave the chest where the king left it. The snake thanks boats with no hooks.",
  ],
  "lk:0": [
    "Sri Lanka, which used to be called Ceylon, has the shape of a teardrop on the map. An island like that asks for big origins, and it has two, not one. Sinhalese children hear both without being made to erase half of the memory. In one, the island is Lanka, belonging to the king Ravana. In the other, it is the land where the prince Vijaya arrived. The ground is the same. It is the books that look from different places. Whoever tells it fairly does not choose one myth to humiliate the other. They put both on the table.",
    "In the Ramayana, Ravana is the ten-headed king, lord of Lanka. Rama, helped by Hanuman, the monkey hero, crosses the sea to reach the island. The journey is not told here as a war full of wounds. It is told as a crossing. There was a serious reason, there was courage, and there was a path over the water. Hanuman leaps and serves as a messenger of hope. Rama keeps his word. Ravana, with all his heads, cannot keep the island for his pride alone. The epic leaves a mark on the landscape that people still trace with a finger on the map.",
    "That mark is a line of stones and sand between India and Sri Lanka. People call it Adam's Bridge, and also Rama's Bridge. On clear days, a photograph from above still shows it, like a pale scratch in the sea. A child can point and say: the legend has geography. It is not just smoke from a story. The stones do not prove every line of the poem, and they need not. They prove that people, long ago, looked at that route and saw a crossing in it. Where the sea is shallow, the imagination rests its foot.",
    "The other origin comes from the Mahavamsa, the ancient chronicle of the Sinhalese. In it, the prince Vijaya arrives from far away and touches this teardrop-shaped land. He founds a people. He brings no ten heads and no army of monkeys. He brings the start of a kingdom, names, and a new idea. The island also explains itself from inside, not only through the neighbouring epic. The two narratives live on the same ground. One links Sri Lanka to the great story of India. The other links Sri Lanka to itself. Erasing either would leave the island half silent.",
    "Without the Ramayana, the bridge would be missing, and so would Ravana. Without the Mahavamsa, Vijaya would be missing, and so would the chronicle. With both, the country is a stage for epic and for written memory. A Sinhalese child can say both names in the same afternoon, beside the map. The stone in the sea, still visible, is the miracle that both sides can point to without arguing. If you look at Sri Lanka, you see the teardrop. You also see the pale scratch between the waters. Geography, treated with respect, gives you back the whole legend.",
  ],
  "lk:1": [
    "Kandy was the last capital of the Sinhalese kings, in the hills of Sri Lanka. In the temple beside the lake, a precious relic is kept: a tooth of the Buddha. For centuries, that tooth was a sacred heart and also a political one. Whoever protected it showed that they could care for the island. Whoever lost it lost more than gold. Once a year, the city fills with the perahera, a procession of drums, fire dancers and elephants dressed in lights. In the middle walks the chosen elephant, with the reliquary held high. The street becomes a circle.",
    "The legend that children hear joins a prince to that elephant. The prince of Kandy had to choose, among several calm animals, which one would carry the relic. He did not choose the biggest just for size. He spent the night near the stable. They say the right elephant dreams the route before walking it. It knows the corners, the lake, and the place where the crowd presses in. The prince saw which one woke without fear and without hurry. It was as though it had already walked the road from inside. He chose that one. The handlers agreed.",
    "If the rite fails, says the legend, the island loses the rain. It is not a threat to make children obey. It is the way of linking care for the temple with care for the land. The prince walked the first lap on foot, behind the elephant. He did not put himself in front of the relic. The drums went into the chest of the city. The dancers' fire drew brief flowers in the dark. The elephant walked as in the dream, sure at every corner. As the reliquary passed, people lowered their heads. The Buddha's tooth went high, small and enormous at once.",
    "The rain, that year in the story, came to the hills at the right time. The rice fields drank, and Kandy put the order of things down to a rite well done. The prince did not keep the glory for himself alone. He said the elephant had dreamed and the musicians had marked the step. The people had opened the street. A kingdom, even an old one, is explained better when it shares the credit. The elephant went back to the stable, still smelling of the sweet smoke of the torches. Next morning it seemed an ordinary animal. Only its eyes kept the map.",
    "The child of Kandy sees the elephant dressed in lights and hears the same link. There is the dream, the rain, the tooth and the street. Without the relic, the old kingdom lost a deep reason to exist. With it, the city still rules the month of the festival, not by force, but by memory. Sri Lanka, in this procession, comes down to four things together: the Buddha, the animal, the rain and the street. If you see the perahera, listen to the drum. Remember the prince who chose for calm. The rain recognises anyone with no hurry to pass ahead of the sacred.",
  ],
  "lk:2": [
    "In the fifth century, in the centre of Sri Lanka, a sugar loaf of rock rises out of the green. It is called Sigiriya. King Kassapa climbed that rock and built a palace on top. He had maidens painted on the walls. He put an enormous lion at the entrance, as if the door of heaven needed a guardian. Whoever climbs today still feels their legs asking for a truce. The view from the top seems too big to fit in your chest. Sri Lanka treats this place as one of its wonders. But the wonder has a story the morning wind cannot wash clean.",
    "The ancient chronicle does not make Kassapa an innocent king. It says he took his father's life. Afterwards he was afraid of his brother. The bare truth is enough. There was a serious crime, and the palace in the sky was also born from that fear. Kassapa climbed up to feel far from the ground where guilt lives. The rock protected him from armies and did not protect him from memory. Children hear this seriously. A beautiful view does not wipe out a wrong. The lovely fresco grew beside a fault that has a name.",
    "The painted maidens still look at whoever passes in the middle of the rock. Time has taken the colours, but not the look. Further down, the lion at the entrance has lost its paws. Time ate them, and the huge stone front paws remained, forming the staircase. It seems an invitation and it seems a warning. Come in, but come in a little humble. A boy climbs with his father and stops there, out of breath. His father says: ‘The sky of this fortress had a price.’ The art stays. The doubt stays too.",
    "Up on top, the wind sweeps the place where there were halls. There is no gold to be seen. There is the map of a man who wanted the sky because he feared the ground. The brother does not disappear from the memory of those who climb. Kassapa could paint maidens and rule from the clouds, and even so he looked down. The legend does not forgive him to make the visit lighter. It does not ask for hatred either. It asks that you see the whole palace: the colour, the height and the guilt. Without that third thing, it is just a viewpoint.",
    "The child comes down with shaking legs and the right story in their ear. Without the doubt, the rock is a postcard. With it, the stone has weight, and weight teaches. The lion with no back legs still asks for a humble entrance. The maidens still look, beautiful and silent, without explaining the king. Sri Lanka can love Sigiriya and, at the same time, not clean up the past. If you climb one day, rest on the terraces and drink water. Remember this: the sky in this fortress was not free. The view is paid for with the truth.",
  ],
  "lk:3": [
    "On the beaches of southern Sri Lanka, near Mirissa, sea turtles come back to lay their eggs. They return to the sand where the species knows the way. The fishermen say that the turtle is a princess who returns. She does not come to rule the village. She comes to keep an old promise to the beach and to stay a while. Then she gives the rest of her life back to the ocean. The island lends the sand during those nights. It does not own the animal. It is the host. Children learn this before they learn the word tourism.",
    "When the hatchlings are born, they look for the shine of the water. If the beach is full of strong lights, the hatchlings get confused. They walk towards the houses, towards the road, far from the sea. Biology confirms what the fishermen already said in their own way. The princess, in the legend, is the name of the rule. The torch goes out. People speak quietly. Nobody puts their body in the way. The darkness, here, is not neglect. It is courtesy. A child on the beach understands the rule better than a speech: if light misleads, lower the light.",
    "On a calm night, the girl Nimali was on the sand with her fisherman grandfather. She carried a small torch, proudly, because the night seemed too big for her. Her grandfather pointed to a tiny movement by the line of eggs. He said: ‘Turn it off.’ She turned it off. Her eyes grew used to the dark. One hatchling, then another, crossed the sand. It was like crossing a whole country with a body the size of a hand. Nobody picked them up to play. Nobody switched on a phone in their faces. The princess needed the dark.",
    "The grandfather said the princess returns because the beach kept her first home. If the village fills the sand with parties and spotlights, she loses her hatchlings on the way. Then the legend becomes sad in a very real way. That is why the fishermen and the wardens all say the same sentence. Low light, step aside, hatchling to the sea. Nimali repeated the sentence until she knew it by heart. It was not a poster. It was kindness towards someone who only borrows the sand for a month and then gives back the horizon.",
    "Without the princess, the rule would be only a warning nailed to a post. With her, the darkness becomes courtesy. The child turns off the light out of love, not only because they are told to. Sri Lanka, land of whales and turtles, lives off this visit today. The legend, if it is honest, does not ask for more photographs. It asks for less shine. The island lends the sand. The turtle lends the wonder and keeps the ocean. If you go to Mirissa on a night like that, keep the torch in your pocket.",
  ],
  "lk:4": [
    "Galle, in the south of Sri Lanka, holds a fortress that the Dutch built by the sea. There are thick walls, a lighthouse, stone streets and old Company doors. For a long time stories talked of gold, of chests and of pirates. Children imagined a treasure under a slab. The elders added something else, less shiny and more useful. The fortress would have a pact with the sea. The sea could roar outside. Inside the stones, it had to charge less. It was a sailors' legend, and nobody imagined it would be tested.",
    "The pact, in the old story, began with a simple agreement. The builders asked the ocean to respect anyone sheltering behind the wall. They promised not to treat the port as an open wound on the coast. It was not wand magic. It was the hope that a well-made wall would talk to the wave. Pirates come into these stories as shadow: they pass, they threaten, and the stone stays. The Company's gold, if it ever sat in boxes, changed hands many times. Galle stayed standing, yellow with sun, looking at the Indian Ocean.",
    "In two thousand and four, a tsunami hit Sri Lanka. This is not a legend to decorate, and it is not told as an adventure. The wave was enormous, the country suffered, and that pain is recent and real. In Galle, the old wall did not erase the sea, but it held back part of the force. Many people ran inside the stones and found shelter. The story's pact said the sea would charge less there. There was no clean miracle and no easy ending. There was a wall doing its old job on a day when that job mattered most.",
    "When the water went down, the treasure that came out of the fortress did not shine. It walked. They were living people, wet, calling each other by name. If there is still gold under some Dutch house, let it stay. The legend, this time, chose well what to keep. The children of Galle hear this chapter in silence. The pirates and the Company become the start of the tale, not the centre. The centre is the stone that held, and the people who could walk again. Nobody wishes to test that pact again.",
    "Without the tsunami, the story of Galle would be only about pirates. With it, the fortress gained a grave chapter, told so that we remember. Sri Lanka teaches two seas there. There is the sea of the Dutch, of ships and trade. There is the sea of two thousand and four, which the wall faced. The child who passes the Company door may still dream of a map. The best map, however, is the one that takes a living person out of the wave. The gold, if there is any, may sleep. The pact has done its work.",
  ],
  "ae:0": [
    "Al Ain, in the emirate of Abu Dhabi, means the spring. Around it, the desert opens out, clear and unhurried. The legend of the oasis's origin tells two things that fit into the same morning. A camel stopped and scraped the sand with its hoof. It refused to go on. A just sheikh saw the gesture and knelt. He prayed that the water, if it came, would not belong to his tent alone. Where the hoof had marked, the sand grew darker. A young palm lifted its head. The country's garden began at that small spot.",
    "Under the sand the water was real, not just from a story. The elders did not wait for a miracle every day. They opened the aflaj, the old channels, and made the water move in turns. It went from garden to garden. Nobody filled a neighbour's jar until it dried up. The palm, with water it could count on, grew. Others came beside it. Shade stopped being luck. It became a plan. A fortress was built to look out over the green. An oasis without a guard turns back into sand.",
    "Al Ain became a real garden, with oasis and fortress heritage. It has palms in rows and walls that can still be visited. The legend of the camel and the prayer sticks to that engineering without wiping it out. The camel showed the place. The prayer remembered justice. The aflaj did the rest, day after day, with hands and with measure. Without the channels, the oasis would be one morning's luck. With the channels, it is skill. The child who walks in the shade today hears both lessons. No one has to choose only one.",
    "Amal went with her grandfather among the palms and asked which of the versions was the right one. He rested his hand on a trunk. ‘Both,’ he said. ‘The camel was thirsty and knew how to stop. The sheikh had power and knew how to share. The channels are the part that is not told with magic. They are the part that holds up the rest.’ Amal drank cool water and looked at the fortress far away. She understood that the desert of the Emirates, without this place, would be only coast and recent history.",
    "The Emirates, in this story, did not begin in the skyscraper. They began in water that knew how to be divided. The palm was the first skyscraper, and it is still standing. Its root is where the legend puts the camel's hoof. Amal learned not to waste a glass. Her grandfather said that every drop still belongs to someone's turn. If the spring has a name, the spring has many owners, not one. The oasis stays green because old justice and the old channel still fit together. Neither one works alone.",
  ],
  "ae:1": [
    "In the desert of the Emirates, the falcon is the brother of the Bedouin. It is trained with patience and loved without sentimentality. It often travels in the front seat of the car, with a leather hood and a place of honour. It is not a shelf ornament. It is eyesight, when the ground lies and all the dunes look the same. A child learns early that the bird is not shouted at. It is invited. The falcon market still shows this respect in small gestures. There is the still glove, the low voice, the bird choosing the moment to land.",
    "The legend of the first falcon begins with a sheikh who was lost. He was no longer sure which way was north. The bird rose and saw further than any man on foot. It guided his eyes to a green patch: the oasis. It did not drag him. It showed him. The sheikh promised, right there, a place in the tent for ever, not only in the hour of fear. A chief's word, in the desert, is either kept or stops being a word. The falcon came down to his fist. The way had a direction.",
    "The sheikh kept his promise. In the tent there was shade, water and a perch. Nobody treated the bird like a tool thrown away after use. It ate, rested, and returned to the sky when it was needed. It did not fly when someone was in a hurry to boast. The years passed. Oil came and changed the cities and the roads. The falcon did not leave the front seat. The Emirates treat it as living heritage, with clubs, care and festivals. But the legend asks for less noise than that. It asks for the old alliance.",
    "At the market, the boy Saif saw a falcon land on his father's glove. It stayed still, its hood still half raised. It seemed to him that the story was there, not in a book. Without the bird, the desert was only ground. With it, the desert has eyes. Saif trained his hand not to tremble. He learned that honour fits into a gesture. Raise the hood in good time. Do not pull the strap in anger. Keep what you said in the morning. His father nodded. ‘If one day you get lost, do not shout at the sand. Raise your eyes.’",
    "The faithful story is the one about the alliance and the word kept. The Emirates, in this tale, are measured in wings and in promises. These promises do not wilt when the thirst passes. The oasis may hide in a dust storm. The falcon still points to it. Saif, later, in the front seat, rode silently beside the travelling cage. He had the respect of someone carrying a brother, not a suitcase. The tent changed shape. The promised place did not change its meaning. Whoever sees from above saves whoever is walking. And whoever was saved does not pretend to have forgotten.",
  ],
  "ae:2": [
    "Sindbad, in this tale from the Gulf, does not set out from a vague map. He sets out from Julfar, in Ras al-Khaimah, or from the old Dubai of fish, before the glass. The boat is a dhow. The possible wealth is pearls. The voyages are seven, a number so round that it already sounds like legend. The Emirates were a coast of diving and sailing. The Gulf was shallow and treacherous, so it asked for a hero who knew how to come back. A child hears the name and sees the triangular sail.",
    "On the first voyage, Sindbad rested on a low island that began to move. It was an enormous whale, asleep at the surface. He did not make it a monster to be wounded. He slid into the water, the dhow came close, and his friends pulled him aboard. Only later was there laughter. On another voyage he saw the Roc, a bird so big that its shadow covered the sail. He did not go near the nest and did not take what was not his. He stayed still. The bird passed and left the whole boat in one piece.",
    "There was also a valley full of shining stones. Sindbad could have filled sacks. He took one only, small, the size of a big pearl, and asked the wind to push him home. The Gulf does not forgive those who want everything at once. The other voyages were of short storms, friendly ports and returns. Seven times he set out. Seven times the coast, when it appeared, was the right one. The pearl divers waved at the sail. He brought back more stories than safes. The stories fitted better in the boat, because they did not rust.",
    "At home, Sindbad sat in the shade and told his tale without turning every trial into a blow. The whale taught him to distrust easy ground. The Roc taught him not to steal what is enormous and ancient. The single stone taught him measure. Someone, laughing, said that seven voyages and seven emirates made a sum too neat to be chance. He shrugged. ‘If the sum helps people remember, let it stay.’ The wind, for its part, needed no sum. It needed someone who knew when to close the sail.",
    "Today an Emirati child, in a pleasure dhow, can still hear the Roc. A sailor tells the story between two buildings of glass. The Emirates do not deny the new coast. They add the boat from before. If Sindbad came back, he would recognise the wind and might not recognise the shore. The tale, though, would recognise the dhow. Seven voyages are enough for a life of returns. The pearl, the bird's shadow and the island that moves still fit into one afternoon. Coming back, in this Gulf, is the true end of every one of them.",
  ],
  "ae:3": [
    "Before oil, the pearl was the Gulf's wage. Divers went down on a single breath, while a song on the boat kept time. The work was hard, and the years at sea went fast. The truth fits into that sentence, without needing a cruel scene. The legend chooses one gesture. In Abu Dhabi they tell of a pearl so perfect that the sea seemed to want it back. The boy Hamad was almost a young man, and he already knew the bottom. He was the one who saw it open in the oyster like a small moon.",
    "On the boat, still panting, he imagined the bidding at the market. Each voice would be louder than the last. The pearl weighed little and was worth a house. Below, the shadow of a shark had crossed the blue. It had gone on, because Hamad had stayed still and come up in time with the song. He did not turn danger into boasting. His father looked at him and did not take the oyster from his hand. ‘You decide,’ he said. ‘The sea heard you go down. It will also hear what you do with this.’",
    "At the market, the biggest buyer smiled and opened the bag of coins. Hamad thought of the houses of the village, the counted breath and his father's sentence. He also thought that a pearl too perfect might be a loan, not a wage. He walked to the edge and gave it back to the sea, without a long speech. Some called him a fool. Some stayed quiet, because silence sometimes recognises a gesture before the counting does. The oyster closed in the shallow water. The day seemed poorer. Hamad did not apologise for not selling.",
    "Next day, the oyster bank gave the village twice the usual amount. It was not a trick to repeat out of greed, hoping for interest. It was the tale saying that the sea pays respect. It also says that the sea leaves empty the hand that only grips. The village divided the catch the way the water turns were divided. Hamad did not become king. He stayed a diver, with the same single breath. The elders began to tell his story without laughing. The song came back to the boat.",
    "In the pearl museum, an Emirati child hears that breath. They see the ropes, the baskets and the map of the bank. Without the divers, many houses would never have begun. With the legend of the returned pearl, there is an ethic beside the memory. The Emirates, rich now, keep this chapter so as not to forget the lungs of those who went down. Greed, in the tale, finds the bank empty. The pearl is judgement. Hamad, white-haired by then, told his grandchildren that the sea is a neighbour, not a safe.",
  ],
  "ae:4": [
    "This is a modern legend, and we say so at once. Nobody should confuse it with a myth from old grandparents. The Burj Khalifa is the tallest tower. The Burj Al Arab is shaped like a sail. The two are folklore of steel, born quickly in a city of sand and glass. Dubai likes stories that grow at the same speed as the scaffolding. Even so, the tale that children hear does not begin with glass. It begins on the beach, with a small drawing and with a father who smelled of fish.",
    "A boy drew a sail in the sand, triangular and stubborn, while he waited for his father's boat. The fisherman looked at the mark before the wave took it. ‘One day,’ he said, ‘there will be a sail that needs no wind.’ His son laughed. In those days, with no wind, the dhow stayed still. The fish stopped being the way to make a living. The sentence seemed impossible. It was the kind parents say to fill the waiting. The wave wiped out the drawing. The boy kept the sentence like a shell.",
    "The years passed. The fishing went on, and then the glass arrived. The city climbed. A hotel shaped like a sail kept the saying in its own way. It can be seen from far off, it shines over the water, and it does not wait for a breeze. The tallest tower kept another part, the height the drawing in the sand could only dream of. Nobody needs to pretend that an engineer copied the child's sketch. The modern legend does not ask for that role. It asks for a line of memory between the fisherman and the concrete.",
    "The Emirati child looks at the Burj and, if someone tells it well, hears the fisherman. Without the old sail, the new one is only a record. With it, the record has a father at sea. The Emirates do not apologise for the glass. They tie it to the dhow, on purpose, so that the height is not left orphaned. The boy of the drawing had grown into a man. He once took his children to the beach and made the same mark. The wave wiped it out again. Behind them, the sail of steel was still in place. He had understood his father.",
    "If the wind fails, the concrete holds, and the city does not stop. The legend, even so, would rather people remembered the wind. Height without memory is only a number in a book of records. With memory, it is a sail. The modern tale ends on the sand, not at the tip of the tower. That is where the sentence was born. Whoever climbs to the viewing deck today can carry a simple idea. Under the glass there is still a dhow, a fish and a father talking. The wiped-out drawing still holds up the story.",
  ],
  "iq:0": [
    "Gilgamesh was king of Uruk, in the land that is now Iraq. At first he ruled without listening, and the city grew tired of him. Then Enkidu arrived, a friend as strong as the king, and the two learned to walk side by side. Without that friend, Gilgamesh stayed a tyrant. With him, he began to be a man. We will not fill the tale with ugly fights. It is enough to say that they crossed the world together and came back different. Children deserve this old story, without a superhero mask.",
    "When Enkidu fell ill and died, as people die, Gilgamesh did not accept the stillness. He left Uruk looking for a life that would not end. The fear of losing more pushed his feet. He crossed difficult lands and spoke with those who guard the passes. He did not turn back. He wanted a remedy against his friend's death, and also against his own. The poem does not treat him as unbeatable on that road. It treats him as someone who loves and does not yet know how to lose. The city waited, with its high walls.",
    "At the end of the road he found Utnapishtim. This old man had survived a great flood inside a boat, with his family and the seeds of the fields. The old man did not mock him. He spoke of a plant at the bottom of the water. It could give back the freshness of youth. Gilgamesh went down and picked the plant. He held it as people hold a last chance. He was tired. On the way back he slept. A snake came near, took the plant, and left on the ground its old skin, now useless. The king woke with empty hands.",
    "There was no shouting at the animal. There was a man sitting in the dust. He understood that eternity did not fit in his pocket. Gilgamesh went back to Uruk. Instead of hiding his shame, he looked at the walls the city had raised with the work of many people. There were wide walls, gates, gardens, the murmur of the streets. That was the eternity that belonged to him. Not a body that never stops. A city that stays, and a friend who had made him better. The plant was lost. The wall was not. He rested his hand on the warm brick.",
    "The clay tablets, written in cuneiform, kept this poem. Iraq is the ground it came from. An Iraqi child can hear the whole thing where it matters: friendship, death, wall. Uruk is the real elixir. The snake is time, which changes its skin and moves on. The king is the one who learns to lose without stopping caring for what he built. If you ever look at an ancient wall and remember a friend, you have already understood Gilgamesh. The immortality that was left over does not shine. It holds things up.",
  ],
  "iq:1": [
    "In the south of Iraq, the Sumerians needed to keep count of sacks of barley. They took damp clay and marked signs with a reed. That is how writing was born. The fact the archaeologists find is already a miracle of patience: the oven, the sign, the sum. The legend adds a teacher who came from the sea. Without that addition, the lesson would be only a date. With it, water teaches too. The tale begins in the morning, when the river is still the colour of copper. People come out to see who is calling from the bank.",
    "Oannes, a gentle fish-man, came out of the water at dawn. He did not come to frighten. He came to show how to count and how to plant. He also showed how to make a sign that would still mean the same thing the next day. Then he went back to the sea. He did not stay to be king. People copied the signs on small tablets and argued less, because the barley now had a number. A child of the village, whom we shall call Nour, learned to mark her own sack. The clay dried in the sun.",
    "Inanna, bright lady of the old stories, comes into this tale, bringing knowledge from hand to hand. We will not go after dark journeys here. What stays is the gift. The signs left the barley behind and began to keep names, laws and songs. Nour understood that the same reed that counted sacks could count her mother's name. The oven baked the tablets so the rain would not undo them. Ur, Babylon and Nineveh fit into this line of clay, each with its kings. But the beginning is humble. It is a sack. It is a scratch.",
    "In the museum, later, an Iraqi child presses her nose to the glass case and sees the tablet. The guide says Oannes, and the sea comes back into the room without wetting the floor. He says Inanna, and knowledge seems like a present that can be passed on. Without the fish-man, only a date would be left in the notebook. With him, the lesson has a tide. Broken clay can still be read. What was baked may crack and still speak. Nour grew up teaching the reed to younger children, always starting with barley.",
    "Iraq gave the world a message that can be held in your hand. Other lands learned the idea and made their own letters. Barley was the first poem. Gilgamesh was the second, when clay already knew how to keep a long story. The hero who does not die, in this tale, is the clay itself. It breaks, it is baked again, and the word stays. If you see an ancient tablet, do not see only a museum object. See the day someone refused to forget a sack. All writing still thanks that small and stubborn gesture.",
  ],
  "iq:2": [
    "The hanging gardens of Babylon are on the list of the seven wonders. Perhaps they existed there. Perhaps they stood in Nineveh. We say the doubt out loud. The ground, if it had them, also swallowed them, and the stones do not show a certificate. The legend is clearer than the map. It speaks of water climbing, green terraces and brick under the leaves. A child of Baghdad hears this and almost feels the drip. In a street that today holds only dust and sun, the tale asks for that image.",
    "It is said that King Nebuchadnezzar had the terraces built for the queen. She came from Media and missed the mountains of her homeland. She did not ask for gold. She asked for shade, cool wind and a slope, even if it had to be made by hand. The builders raised wide steps. Water climbed from channel to channel, with patient skill, until the plants stood above the dust. The queen walked there and breathed air that reminded her of home. The love in this story is care. It turned homesickness into shade, not a pretty prison.",
    "The Greeks spread the wonder through their books, and the world went on imagining a forest in the air. Nobody, in this tale for children, needs a war plan to understand the garden. They need the queen at the window, before the work. She looks at the flat horizon and says that she misses the mountain. Nebuchadnezzar could have offered jewels. He offered roots. The gardeners went up and down with watering cans and seedlings. The brick, warm, learned to be forest floor. If a leaf fell into the street, people looked up and smiled without seeing the top.",
    "If the gardens were real, the Iraqi ground kept them in its own way, which is also losing them. The doubt does not spoil the story. We sit it beside the beauty. The girl Huda, in Baghdad, always draws a dripping terrace. She does it even when the teacher says it might have been somewhere else. ‘If the stones are missing, homesickness holds up the plants,’ she answers, repeating her grandmother. Without the queen, the wonder would be only skill. With her, the skill is love with roots. The first architect was not the king.",
    "Iraq, in this legend, taught the world to hang a forest. It may be Babylon. It may be Nineveh. It may be the place where one person's homesickness changes the shape of a city. Children do not need the missing stone to understand care. They need the image of water climbing up to the leaf. Huda waters a plant on the balcony and says it is a small hanging garden, with only one pot. Her grandmother does not laugh. All wonder began with someone wanting shade for another person. The rest is height, channel and patience.",
  ],
  "iq:3": [
    "The Tigris and the Euphrates cross the land of Iraq like two brothers. Between them lies the cradle the Greeks called Mesopotamia. The word only means this: the land between rivers. One myth says the brothers were born from a gift of the gods. Dilmun lay around them. So did that garden some old maps treat as a beginning of the world. A child sees the two lines on the map and thinks of two plaits. Without them, it would be desert. With them, mud can become bread. The tale and geography sit in the same bed.",
    "Once, says the legend, the brothers argued about who could run faster. While they ran, the fields dried up and the wheat did not lift its head. An old voice, perhaps coming from Dilmun, asked them to walk side by side. They listened. The mud returned. The first wheat rose between two waters that had stopped competing. The flood still came, because a living river rises and falls. People learned to move the grain elsewhere and to count the sacks. Legend and archaeology, once again, get their feet wet in the same place.",
    "From that cradle came things the world uses: the wheel, writing, law. Hammurabi had rules carved so that the weak and the strong could point to the same words. It was not a river, but it was born between rivers. There, the count of barley had already taught that memory needs a shape. One brother alone was not enough for the cradle. The Tigris without the Euphrates would be a line. The two together make a lap of earth. Villages grew on the banks, and boats learned both temperaments. Children learned the two names first.",
    "Baghdad drinks from the Tigris. It owes the same honour to the Euphrates, even when the city looks mostly at one bank. The boy Karim asked his grandfather which of the brothers was more important. His grandfather refused the choice. ‘You look after both,’ he said. ‘A cradle with one leg rocks nobody.’ They took a simple map into the kitchen. His grandfather ran a finger along the two lines until they almost touched. Karim understood that folklore, here, is sacred geography. It does not ask for a monster. It asks that nobody forgets half the water.",
    "The flood is still a warning, and the mud is still a gift, if a person treats it with measure. The two brothers of the legend do not need a new race. They need someone who does not pick a favourite and let the other dry up. Iraq is the land between them, and the old name says only that, and says it justly. Karim, at school, draws the two plaits before he draws the house. If one is missing, he starts the page again. Outside the two lines, the desert waits.",
  ],
  "iq:4": [
    "On Baghdad nights Ali Baba still opens, with the souk as his theatre. He was a poor woodcutter. Hidden behind baskets, he heard robbers say in front of a rock: ‘Open, sesame.’ The stone obeyed. Inside there was treasure. Ali Baba waited for them to leave and went in. He took only a small sack, what the house needed for flour and for debts. The rock closed. He did not boast in the square. The city of peace, with its minarets, likes this measure. The right word had opened. Good sense had limited it.",
    "His brother Cassim learned of the gold and wanted the whole cave. He shouted the words, filled too many sacks, and forgot the words to get out. The door closed with him in the dark, terrified. There is not a drop of blood in this tale for children. Ali Baba went back, said ‘Open, sesame’ and pulled him out, pale and ashamed. Greed had closed what the word had opened. Cassim promised not to go back alone. For a few days he even kept to it. In the souk, though, a coin fell from a sack.",
    "The chief marked Ali Baba's door with a sign. Morgiana, the servant, saw the mark and did not wait for fear to arrive. She was the bright head of the house, the true strength of the home, without needing a sword. She put the same sign on every door in the street. The robbers stopped, confused, and went away. They tried again, disguised as merchants, with big jars in the courtyard. In almost every jar sat a silent man. Morgiana heard a whisper and did not go close alone. She told Ali Baba: ‘The house is not on its own.’ Quick thinking was the first lock.",
    "Together they lit lanterns and called the neighbours and the night watch. Many eyes are worth more than one fight. The story does not need an ugly scene for the house to be saved. The men came out of the jars and ran away with empty hands. The gold that was left paid for the bread and for Morgiana's share, which nobody forgot. Cassim did not go back to the rock. He had learned that a room full of coins, without the word and without measure, is a trap. Ali Baba kept the sentence to tell the children quietly, like a key.",
    "Harun al-Rashid, the caliph of the stories, liked to walk through Baghdad in disguise at night, listening to the city. He heard Morgiana tell of what one word can open and what greed closes. Ali Baba, Morgiana and Harun are three ways of crossing the night. There is the honest poor man, the watchful servant, and the king who listens. The legend leaves the servant as the winner. Baghdad, even after hard years, keeps this ancient right. It is still the place where one sentence opens a door. If you say ‘Open, sesame’ in the souk, someone smiles.",
  ],
  "il:0": [
    "In the Valley of Elah, in Israel, the soldiers were afraid of an enormous Philistine warrior called Goliath. He shouted from the other side of the stream and nobody wanted to take a step. David was the youngest at home. He was a shepherd, and he had only gone to bring bread and cheese to his brothers. He heard the challenge and said he would face the giant. He was not the tallest and he was not the most armed. He was the one who would not keep quiet while the valley shook with that voice.",
    "King Saul wanted to dress him in heavy armour, of iron and leather. David tried it on, took two steps and shook his head. It was far too big for his arm. He took off the iron. He kept the shepherd's sling, which some people also call a catapult. He went down to the edge of the water and chose five smooth stones, the right size. He put them in his bag. He did not invent a new weapon. He carried what he already knew how to use when he guarded the sheep.",
    "Goliath laughed to see a boy with no sword. David answered that he did not come leaning on iron. He came leaning on the name of the God of Israel, the same one who guarded him in the pasture. He put a stone in the sling, spun it, and let go. The stone hit the giant, and Goliath fell to the ground and did not get up. There is no need to tell more than that. The Philistine army saw him fall and lost its courage. It fled down the valley. Fear had changed sides.",
    "That day the shepherd did not suddenly win a throne. But the road began there, among the stones of the stream. The people began to tell the story from generation to generation. Even today some point to the Valley of Elah and say: it was on this ground. The children of Israel hear David as they hear the Scriptures, not as a loose fairy tale. The small one does not win by film magic. He wins because he chooses the right measure. He does not borrow someone else's strength, and he does not wait to be given it.",
    "Without the stone, the boy was only courage waiting. With the stone, and with the Name he trusted, the giant fell. Israel remembers, in this story, a youngster who refused armour too heavy for his body. The sling was enough. If one day you are handed armour that is not yours, have the sense to put it down. The strength that fits in your hand, used honestly, can be greater than the loudest shout in the valley. The valley is quiet again, and the stream keeps running. It is a good place to remember.",
  ],
  "il:1": [
    "Jonah was a prophet. He received a clear order: go to the city of Nineveh and warn the people that their path was crooked. He did not want to. He was afraid. He also did not fancy that far and enemy city being able to change. Instead of obeying, he went down to a port and boarded a ship going the other way. He thought the sea would hide him. The boat moved away from land. Jonah went down to the hold, looking for sleep. He slept badly.",
    "A storm rose so strong that the sailors no longer knew which god to ask for help. They were people from several countries. Frightened, Jonah told the truth. The storm had come because of his escape. He asked them to throw him into the sea, so that the ship would be saved. They resisted and rowed harder. At last they did what he asked. The waves grew calm. An enormous fish came near and swallowed the prophet. Jonah stayed three days in that dark, alive, without seeing the sky.",
    "Inside, with no ground but the belly of the fish, Jonah prayed. He did not arrange pretty excuses. He spoke as someone who can no longer run to another port. The fish obeyed a greater order and left him on a beach, panting, once again under the sun. Jonah got up and went to Nineveh, still unwilling. He warned the city. To his astonishment, the people listened. They changed their lives and asked for mercy. The city was not destroyed. The warning had done its work in a way he did not expect.",
    "Jonah, instead of being glad, was angry. He had announced an end, and the end had not come. He sat outside the walls, waiting. He almost wanted to be right instead of seeing people saved. Then a plant grew that gave him cool shade, and he liked it. The next day the plant dried up, and the sun beat on his head. He complained loudly. He was sadder about the lost shade than happy that a whole city had turned back. His anger came out in the heat.",
    "The lesson came in that heat. If Jonah cared about a plant he had not planted, how could there not be mercy for Nineveh? There were so many people and so many animals there. The message is bigger than the fish. The sea, the animal and the city all fit into the story. But the centre is the messenger's stubbornness. Mercy does not ask permission from whoever carries the warning. If you are sent to help, do not run away on the first boat. The second chance waits for you.",
  ],
  "il:2": [
    "Solomon was king in Jerusalem, and one day he could ask for whatever he wanted. He did not ask for gold, or a long life just for himself. He asked for wisdom to rule the people with justice. The request was heard, and the king's fame travelled beyond the sands. In the kingdom of Sheba, a powerful queen heard about him and was not satisfied with rumours. She gathered gifts, camels and difficult questions. Then she set off to see with her own eyes. She wanted to test the stories.",
    "In Jerusalem, the queen did not begin with the celebration. She began with riddles. She wanted to know whether the wisdom was true or only a story well told. She asked questions that confused the wise men of the court. Solomon answered them one by one, calmly, without mocking the person who asked. He showed her the order of the house, the table and the work of the temple. He showed her the way a case was heard before a judgement was given. The queen was silent for a long time.",
    "Then she spoke. She said she had not believed the news until she saw it. The truth was even greater than the report. She offered gold, stones and fragrant spices. Solomon gave presents too, as the hospitality of a king requires. She did not stay to live in Jerusalem. She went back to her own land, taking what she had seen. The Bible tells of this visit, the astonishment and the offerings. Other lands, such as Ethiopia, stretch the tale out. Here the visit is what stays.",
    "The temple she spoke of was destroyed later, and even so it lives on in this memory. Without the queen, Solomon might be just one king among kings. With her, the knowledge was examined by someone from outside, who had no obligation to applaud. Israel, in this story, honours the question. A wise answer opens respect, the way water opens an oasis. Vanity, on the other hand, does not hold up a temple for ever. Even the wise king knew that houses of stone come to an end.",
    "Children hear the riddles and understand the essentials. They do not need the whole list. It matters less to memorise each question than to see a powerful person travel just to confirm the truth. If you know something, let people ask you. Wisdom that refuses examination stays small. The queen went home, and the king kept his old request. He had asked not for gold, but for good judgement. The temple fell. The visit, told with care, is still standing. Its questions are still useful.",
  ],
  "il:3": [
    "In the Sinai desert, the people Moses was leading grew so thirsty that their courage dried up. There was sun, rock and sand, and almost no shade. The people complained. They had come out of a land of hard work, and now freedom seemed to be only dust. Moses took the complaint seriously. He did not pretend the thirst was small. Among the rocks came the order to bring water out of a place where nobody expected a cup. He struck the rock, as he had been told.",
    "The water gushed out. It was not a timid trickle. It was enough to drink for the people and for the animals to stop trembling. The desert, which had seemed shut, opened a liquid door. The children drank first. The elders filled whatever they had to hand. In Israel, and in the dry lands of the Negev, people still teach that water is a treasure. It is not leftovers to be dropped on the ground. Each drop has to be carried home, and nothing is poured away.",
    "Further on the thirst came back, because the desert is not crossed in one afternoon. Again the people shouted, and again the rock was on the way. The order, this time, asked for trust in the word. Moses was tired and angry at so much complaining. He struck the rock twice, with the force of someone who has run out of patience. The water came out all the same, and everyone drank. The gift was not taken back. But Moses's haste and anger did not go unnoticed.",
    "The price was this: he would see the promised land from far away and would not enter it. He had led the people through the desert. He had asked the rock for water. Even so, a failure weighed on him. The story for children does not hide that side. The miracle and the mistake go together, like sun and shade on the sand. Without the water, the crossing stopped. With the water, and with the mistake, the journey has a cost. Obeying is not cheap when your throat burns.",
    "Israel is also born, in this memory, from a cup taken out of the rock. The desert is the teacher, and the water is the lesson that is not wasted. If you are thirsty and are given a drink, do not splash the rest about for fun. And if you are tired, do not swap the right word for two angry blows. The Negev is still dry and beautiful. Whoever crosses it learns quickly what the tale already said: every drop counts, and every order does too. Both lessons travel with you.",
  ],
  "il:4": [
    "When the waters of the flood covered the earth, Noah was in the ark. His family and the animals were with him, and they waited for a sign. The rain had stopped, but the world was still a lake with no edges. He released a dove. It flew, found nowhere to land, and came back. Noah waited. The second time, the dove returned with an olive leaf in its beak, green and tender. That meant dry land, a place where a tree had started to breathe again.",
    "The leaf was small, and even so it changed the air inside the ark. There was hope in the shape of a branch. Later the dove did not come back, because it had found ground. Noah understood that the world was starting again. The story links that leaf to peace and to the covenant. It is a promise that life would not be wiped out again by a sea without end. Without the dove, Noah did not know whether he could open the door. With it, the covenant gained a leaf.",
    "The olive tree, in Israel and in Palestine, is not just a drawing in that tale. It is the tree of the house, planted beside people. It can live for centuries if nobody cuts it down on a whim. It gives olives, it gives shade, and it gives an oil that goes with bread. The biblical legend and country life meet in this branch. The dove carries the message. The olive stays in daily life, on the table and in the harvest. It also stays in the names of lands where grandparents worked.",
    "There are schools where a child plants a small olive tree and learns to water it slowly. It is not a decoration for one day. It is a way of saying that peace needs roots. The branch in the dove's beak does not finish the story by itself. It needs someone to receive it. It needs someone who does not cut down the tree that proves it. The leaf announces firm ground. The grown tree announces that this ground was cared for long enough to give fruit. Both messages need a gardener.",
    "Telling this faithfully means remembering the flood, the ark and the branch. It also means not jumping to the pretty ending too fast. The earth gave its warning in green. Israel, with its old olive groves, lives the symbol when it harvests and when it protects the tree. Peace, in that leaf, still needs someone to carry it from one bank to the other. It also needs someone who does not cut the trunk because they are in a hurry. An old olive tree is not any old wood. It has already been a message.",
  ],
};

export default PART;
